Story Content: {'story': 'On a rainy Tuesday morning, Sarah discovered an old letter hidden in her grandmother’s attic. Sarah turned the envelope over. On the back, in her grandmother’s small, careful handwriting, was one word: Ruth. Before she could read the letter, Bix came nosing up the attic stairs, sneezed hard at the dust, and backed down two steps as if the whole place had personally offended him. Sarah laughed under her breath, then noticed the envelope was addressed to someone her family had never mentioned. She slid a finger beneath the flap.\n\nSarah unfolded the letter, careful not to tear the brittle crease. “If you’ve found this, you’ve probably found the blue cup, too,” it began. Behind her, her grandmother’s old tabby, Button, shoved his nose beneath a loose strip of carpet and sneezed dust across the floorboards. Sarah looked down: one board sat slightly higher than the others. The letter went on, “Don’t let your mother take it home.”\n\nSarah read the sentence twice. From the stairwell came Bix’s nails, then his broad head appeared around the hatch, a strip of carpet fringe stuck to one whisker. “Not now,” she whispered, though he nudged her hand as if there might be a snack in it. She eased the raised board up with the brass edge of a picture frame. Beneath it sat a blue cup wrapped in a dish towel, and tucked inside the cup was a small photograph: her mother, much younger, standing beside a woman Sarah had never seen. On the back, someone had written, “Ruth was right to keep it.”\n\nSarah eased the page open. At the top, in the same careful hand, was “Ruth—”. Beneath the warning about the cup, her grandmother had written, “I kept it here because you asked me to. I didn’t know if you’d come back.” Sarah’s thumb paused on that last sentence. From beneath the raised floorboard came a soft, hollow clink. Button nosed at the seam again while Bix watched from the stairs.\n\nSarah lifted the board a little farther. A small brass key had caught in a notch underneath, its ring wrapped in a faded strand of blue thread. Button pawed at it once; Bix gave a low, uncertain whine from the stairs. Then her mother called up from below, “Sarah? Did you find anything up there?” Sarah closed her fingers around the key. The letter’s last line—*I didn’t know if you’d come back*—seemed to change shape in her mind. She could answer honestly. Or she could ask first, “Who’s Ruth?”\n\nSarah turned the photograph toward the gray window light. Her mother’s arm rested around the stranger’s shoulders, but she wasn’t looking at the camera; she was looking at the woman, smiling in a way Sarah had never seen in any family picture. Button put one paw on the dish towel, and the cup gave a small, unsteady rattle. Sarah caught it before it tipped. On the underside, beneath a brown ring of age, someone had scratched two letters: R.H. She looked from them to the name on the envelope. For a moment, she considered taking the photograph downstairs. Instead, she folded the letter over it, as if that might keep the question from spreading.'}
